# Story report

1. What the runs prove

Each claim below is checked over the flushed files; the verdict table (section 7) says whether it held.
An assertion checks one component's own numbers; an oracle check compares the proxy's numbers with an
independent record (the simulated clients, the fake Postgres or the load tool).

The report's structural checks:
- DuckDB: DuckDB is installed and is the pinned family 1.5.x (it reads the files)
- arguments: every argument is an existing directory
- runs: at least one run directory was found
- finished: every run directory has run.csv (the run finished)
- files: every run directory has metrics.csv and histograms.csv
- schema header: every file starts with the schema version 1 header
- schema guard: the complete runs pass test/analysis/guard.sql: schema version 1 in every row, no empty required field, one run.csv row and a distinct run id per directory, metric rows in every run

Scenario e2e-queue-full:
- every metric its checks require is in the final flush of every run
- oracle completed.sql: e2e-queue-full: two components, one query: the proxy received exactly the requests the clients sent, and the requests it completed (relayed to their end marker) are the clients' successes plus the errors the database itself returned.
- assertion conservation.sql: e2e-queue-full: conservation per instance from the exported counters: received = accepted + rejected, accepted = completed + failed + shed, and queued and in flight are 0 in the final flush; something was received.
- assertion faults.sql: e2e-queue-full: more clients than connections plus queue: exactly the three over the bound were shed (53M01).
- assertion resets.sql: e2e-queue-full: resets completed + resets failed + connections closed at release + connections lent as is = leases ended = leases started; resets started = leases ended with a reset; nothing is resetting in the final flush.
- oracle rows.sql: e2e-queue-full: every proxy-made error a client saw is counted under its error-contract row, and every counted row for a request reached a client: shed = 53M01, unsupported-feature + unsupported-extended = 0A000, lost-after-read = 08006, lost-after-write = 08007.

Scenario e2e-reject-list:
- every metric its checks require is in the final flush of every run
- oracle completed.sql: e2e-reject-list: two components, one query: the proxy received exactly the requests the clients sent, and the requests it completed (relayed to their end marker) are the clients' successes plus the errors the database itself returned.
- assertion conservation.sql: e2e-reject-list: conservation per instance from the exported counters: received = accepted + rejected, accepted = completed + failed + shed, and queued and in flight are 0 in the final flush; something was received.
- oracle faults.sql: e2e-reject-list: the fake Postgres started only the forwarded statements and the resets: nothing refused reached it.
- assertion resets.sql: e2e-reject-list: resets completed + resets failed + connections closed at release + connections lent as is = leases ended = leases started; resets started = leases ended with a reset; nothing is resetting in the final flush.
- oracle rows.sql: e2e-reject-list: every proxy-made error a client saw is counted under its error-contract row, and every counted row for a request reached a client: shed = 53M01, unsupported-feature + unsupported-extended = 0A000, lost-after-read = 08006, lost-after-write = 08007.

Scenario e2e-reset-error:
- every metric its checks require is in the final flush of every run
- oracle completed.sql: e2e-reset-error: two components, one query: the proxy received exactly the requests the clients sent, and the requests it completed (relayed to their end marker) are the clients' successes plus the errors the database itself returned.
- assertion conservation.sql: e2e-reset-error: conservation per instance from the exported counters: received = accepted + rejected, accepted = completed + failed + shed, and queued and in flight are 0 in the final flush; something was received.
- assertion faults.sql: e2e-reset-error: the one tampered reset reply fails the reset with reason error and closes its connection.
- assertion resets.sql: e2e-reset-error: resets completed + resets failed + connections closed at release + connections lent as is = leases ended = leases started; resets started = leases ended with a reset; nothing is resetting in the final flush.
- oracle rows.sql: e2e-reset-error: every proxy-made error a client saw is counted under its error-contract row, and every counted row for a request reached a client: shed = 53M01, unsupported-feature + unsupported-extended = 0A000, lost-after-read = 08006, lost-after-write = 08007.

Scenario e2e-results:
- every metric its checks require is in the final flush of every run
- oracle completed.sql: e2e-results: two components, one query: the proxy received exactly the requests the clients sent, and the requests it completed (relayed to their end marker) are the clients' successes plus the errors the database itself returned.
- assertion conservation.sql: e2e-results: conservation per instance from the exported counters: received = accepted + rejected, accepted = completed + failed + shed, and queued and in flight are 0 in the final flush; something was received.
- assertion resets.sql: e2e-results: resets completed + resets failed + connections closed at release + connections lent as is = leases ended = leases started; resets started = leases ended with a reset; nothing is resetting in the final flush.
- oracle rows.sql: e2e-results: every proxy-made error a client saw is counted under its error-contract row, and every counted row for a request reached a client: shed = 53M01, unsupported-feature + unsupported-extended = 0A000, lost-after-read = 08006, lost-after-write = 08007.

Scenario load-errors:
- every metric its checks require is in the final flush of every run
- assertion report:loadgen-answered: load tool conservation, per instance in the final flush: every sent request has a final outcome or is still in flight (sent = completed over ok, db-error, refused and lost + in_flight).
- assertion report:loadgen-conservation: load tool conservation, per instance in the final flush: every intended request was sent, could not be sent, or is still queued (intended = sent + could_not_send + queued).
- assertion report:loadgen-drained: load tool: nothing is left queued in the final flush (queued = 0).
- assertion report:loadgen-labels: load tool label contract: completed is exported for exactly the four outcomes ok, db-error, refused and lost, and every errors_by_class reason is a two-character SQLSTATE class.
- assertion report:loadgen-moved: load tool: counters must move; every run intended at least one request (intended > 0).

Scenarios with runs and no assertions (shown, neither a pass nor a failure): demo-no-sql

2. How the data was collected

- Tiers: R (real: the real binary or tool against a real Postgres), 1 runs, 2 flushes; S (simulator: the real engine on the event loop with the fake Postgres and simulated clients), 13 runs, 30 flushes.
- Schema version: 1 (every file header and every row; any other version fails the report).
- Runs: 14 complete run directories of 14 found under 1 argument(s), 6 scenarios; 32 flushes in all, 2 to 3 per run; 1 to 1 instances per run.
- Rows: 19093 metric rows and 1511 histogram bucket rows.
- Components in the final flushes: classifier, engine, fakepg, loadgen, obs, sim, simnet, wire, workload.
- Source: each run directory holds the CSV flush of PLAN.md section 7.7 (metrics.csv, histograms.csv, run.csv;
  rows appended at every publish interval and once more at the end, run.csv written last). The files are read
  with DuckDB 1.5.x through test/analysis/views.sql; "final flush" is the last flush_seq of a run. A run's
  scenario is the scenario column of its run.csv.

3. Inputs

| scenario        | tier | runs | seeds | arms | flushes | instances |
|-----------------|------|------|-------|------|---------|-----------|
| demo-no-sql     | S    | 1    | 1     | base | 3       | 1         |
| e2e-queue-full  | S    | 3    | 1-3   | base | 6       | 1         |
| e2e-reject-list | S    | 3    | 1-3   | base | 6       | 1         |
| e2e-reset-error | S    | 3    | 1-3   | base | 9       | 1         |
| e2e-results     | S    | 3    | 1-3   | base | 6       | 1         |
| load-errors     | R    | 1    | 42    | base | 2       | 1         |

Legend: one row per scenario (run.csv scenario), aggregated over its runs. tier: S simulator, R real, bench.
runs: complete run directories. seeds: lowest-highest seed. arms: the arms present. flushes: flushes written,
summed over the runs. instances: instances per run (the distinct values).

